<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nakbe</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nakbe is a Maya archaeological site in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirador-basin">Mirador Basin</a> of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, about 13 kilometres south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-mirador">El Mirador</a>. One of the earliest Maya cities, it had large platforms and temples in the Middle Preclassic period, from about 800 BCE, among the oldest monumental buildings in the Maya lowlands, and it was linked to El Mirador by a causeway. It declined in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-preclassic">Late Preclassic</a> period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nakbe.jpg" alt="Nakbe" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Simon Burchell · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nakbe,_small_temple_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Excavations suggest that people were living at Nakbe by the Early Formative period, about 1400 BCE. By the 8th century BCE it had monumental architecture, with some platforms 18 metres high, and earlier villages were levelled and filled to serve as foundations for the new buildings, so that the platforms and temples were planned together. The buildings fall into an East Group and a West Group of the formative periods. The largest is Structure 1, a pyramid flanked by two large stucco masks and crowned with three buildings in the triadic style.</p><p>Raised causeways paved with crushed white stone, the sacbeob or white ways, linked the parts of the city; the Kan Causeway stood 4 metres above the ground in places. Strombus shells, among the earliest major imports into the interior of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, exotic goods such as jade and obsidian, and human incisors inlaid with jadelike stone from about 2,800 years ago, point to growing differences of rank. Archaeologist Richard D. Hansen has argued that the demand for such imports may have required administrative and governmental organisations here earlier than elsewhere.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The site was first seen in aerial photographs in 1930, and Ian Graham began the first excavations in 1962. Large-scale work followed in the 1980s and 1990s under the RAINPEG Project, a joint effort of the Institute of Geophysics and Planetary Sciences of UCLA and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>'s Institute of Anthropology and History, directed by Hansen. A main aim was the limestone quarries: the team recovered 23 chert tools, among them bifacial picks and axes and hammerstones, and made replicas to learn how the Maya cut blocks from the bedrock and shaped them.</p><p>Nakbe Stela 1, smashed in antiquity into forty-five fragments, once stood 3.4 metres tall. Reassembled, it shows two figures face to face, one pointing to a disembodied head, a scene interpreted as the supernatural twins Hun-Apu and Ixbalanque of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popol-vuh">Popol Vuh</a>. After a thousand years of near abandonment, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-classic-period">Late Classic Maya</a> reoccupied the site, settling around the ruins and building the Codex Group. They raised no monuments, but left fine Classic ceramics, among them the Princeton Vase.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nakbe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Nakbe</b> had large platforms and temples by about 800 BCE, and El Mirador, at its height from the 3rd century BCE, covered some 16 square kilometres; its La Danta complex rises about 72 metres above the forest floor, and raised causeways linked the city to Nakbe and its…</blockquote><a href="/culture/maya-civilisation">Maya civilisation · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Stucco masks of deities decorated platform façades, and raised causeways (sakbeob) linked El Mirador with <b>Nakbe</b>, Tintal and other sites, suggesting a regional state.</blockquote><a href="/state/el-mirador">El Mirador</a></li></ul></section>
